<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rupert of Salzburg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 710</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Saint Rupert</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rupert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a> was a Frankish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> and missionary, probably from Worms, who worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> in the late 7th and early 8th centuries. Invited by Duke Theodo, he founded a church and the monastery of St Peter at the ruined Roman town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iuvavum">Iuvavum</a> around 696, and the convent of Nonnberg. Venerated as a saint, he is the patron of the city and state of Salzburg.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rupert-of-salzburg.jpg" alt="Rupert of Salzburg" width="273" height="300" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rupert-Salzburg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rupert was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Worms as well as the first Bishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a> and abbot of St Peter's Abbey there, and a contemporary of the Frankish king Childebert III. Holy tradition holds that he came from the Frankish royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merovingian-dynasty">Merovingian dynasty</a>. He was possibly related to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robertians">Robertians</a> and is likely a descendant of Count Palatine Chrodbert II.</p><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> at Worms he was at first accepted as a wise and devout dignitary, but the mostly pagan community came to reject him and forced him out of the city by the end of the 7th century. Saints Chuniald and Gislar accompanied him in his missionary work, though no records of their acts survive. The Agilolfing duke Theodo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> then asked him to come to his residence at Regensburg to help spread the Christian faith among the Bavarian tribes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Rupert moved to Altötting and began by preaching to the locals. He sailed down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, visiting many towns, villages and forts, and converted a large population as far southeast as the Bavarian border with the Pannonian lands, then under the Avar Khaganate. He stayed at Lorch, the former Roman city of Lauriacum, where an early Christian church already existed. Warlike conditions in the borderlands made him give up plans for a mission among the Pannonian Avars.</p><p>He instead went by the Roman road to the ruined city of Juvavum, which he made his base and renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a>. He re-established the monastic community at St Peter's Abbey and laid the foundations of Salzburg Cathedral, finished by his successor Vergilius. He also founded the Benedictine nunnery of Nonnberg, where his niece Erentrude became the first abbess. From Theodo his bishopric received estates around Piding and Reichenhall, where he promoted the saltworks, and he introduced higher education and other reforms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Rupert reportedly died on Easter Sunday around 710, though other sources say he returned to Worms and died there. Vergilius moved his remains to Salzburg Cathedral on 24 September 774. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Eastern Orthodox Church</a> keeps his feast on 27 March, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> on 24 September, the day his relics arrived. Rupertitag is a public holiday in the state of Salzburg. He is patron saint of the state and of the Archdiocese of Salzburg together with Vergilius, is known as the Apostle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavarians">Bavarians</a>, and is patron of settlements such as Sankt Ruprecht in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a> and Šentrupert in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rupert of Salzburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Saint Rupert</b> founded a church and monastery at the Roman town of Iuvavum around 696, and Salzburg became an archbishopric in 798.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-archbishopric-of-salzburg">Prince-Archbishopric of Salzburg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Worms, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.63,8.36&amp;year=700" aria-label="Worms on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4742 435 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Rupert of Salzburg"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5192 705h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5192 705h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.63,8.36&amp;year=700">What was here in 700 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
